The mathematical tool needed is the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality. The lecture approaches it through elementary vector geometry: the dot product of two vectors is their lengths multiplied by the cosine of the angle between them, so its magnitude cannot exceed the product of their lengths. In Hilbert-space notation, $\braket{A}{A}\braket{B}{B}
\geq
\left|\braket{A}{B}\right|^2.$ It holds in real or complex vector spaces of any dimension.

