p.src:
The remaining inner product is $\begin{aligned}
\braket{A}{B}
&=
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
dx\,
x\psi(x)
\left(-i\hbar\frac{d\psi}{dx}\right).\end{aligned}$ Only its absolute value enters. Since $\psi$ is real, $\psi\frac{d\psi}{dx}
=
\frac{1}{2}\frac{d}{dx}\psi^2.$ Therefore, $\begin{aligned}
\left|\braket{A}{B}\right|
&=
\frac{\hbar}{2}
\left|
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
dx\,
x\frac{d}{dx}\psi^2
\right| \notag\\
&=
\frac{\hbar}{2}
\left|
-\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
dx\,\psi^2
\right|
=
\frac{\hbar}{2},
\end{aligned}$ where integration by parts removes the derivative from $\psi^2$, $d x/dx=1$, the boundary term vanishes, and normalization supplies the last equality.

